 a pre-release 0.x version number. Today we roll over to 1.0.0! So: what’s changed?\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"structure-and-stability\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Structure and stability\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#structure-and-stability\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Structure and stability\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeLinecap\":\"square\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"strokeWidth\":1.4,\"d\":\"m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75\"}]}]}]]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The first thing that’s changed is the structure of the code. The serverless driver emerged somewhat organically out of initial experiments in using \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/blog/serverless-driver-for-postgres\",\"children\":\"standalone WebSocket-to-TCP proxies and userspace TLS\"}],\". The upshot of this was that the repo was rather oddly structured; the bundled TypeScript types (i.e.\",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\".d.ts\"}],\" files) were generated and maintained by hand; and there were no conventional tests (although there was a bunch of code that exercised various features of the driver). Over time, these things became bit of an impediment to progress, both Neon-developed improvements and perhaps also contributions from outside.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"So version 1.0.0 of the driver is published from \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/neondatabase/serverless\",\"children\":\"a heavily refactored codebase\"}],\", which has a more normal and familiar structure; the types are now extracted and bundled up automatically (using Microsoft’s \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://api-extractor.com/\",\"children\":\"api-extractor\"}],\"); and there’s a decent set of CI tests to catch any future regressions. That includes testing on Node, Bun, Deno, Firefox, Chrome, Cloudflare Workers and Vercel Functions, and via adapters for the Prisma and Drizzle ORMs.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"queries-over-http\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Queries over HTTP\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#queries-over-http\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Queries over HTTP\",\"children\":\"$L61\"}]]}],\"\\n\",\"$L62\",\"\\n\",\"$L63\",\"\\n\",\"$L64\",\"\\n\",\"$L65\",\"\\n\",\"$L66\",\"\\n\",\"$L67\",\"\\n\",\"$L68\",\"\\n\",\"$L69\",\"\\n\",\"$L6a\",\"\\n\",\"$L6b\",\"\\n\",\"$L6c\",\"\\n\",\"$L6d\",\"\\n\",\"$L6e\",\"\\n\",\"$L6f\",\"\\n\",\"$L70\",\"\\n\",\"$L71\",\"\\n\",\"$L72\",\"\\n\",\"$L73\",\"\\n\",\"$L74\",\"\\n\",\"$L75\",\"\\n\",\"$L76\",\"\\n\",\"$L77\",\"\\n\",\"$L78\",\"\\n\",\"$L79\",\"\\n\",\"$L7a\",\"\\n\",\"$L7b\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"61:[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$57:6:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]\n62:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The other developments in 1.0.0 all relate to queries carried over HTTP fetch. Two of these are enhancements, and one of these is breaking change we felt it was worth making for the sake of security.\"}]\n63:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"tagged-template-composability\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Tagged-template composability\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#tagged-template-composability\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Tagged-template composability\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$57:6:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n64:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The main interface for HTTP queries in our driver is a JavaScript tagged-template function, which makes it easy to safely interpolate user-supplied values. It works like this:\"}]\n66:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/blog/sql-template-tags\",\"children\":\"The reason this is safe\"}],\" is that untrusted values are not directly interpolated into the SQL string. Instead, we insert numbered placeholders — \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"$$1\"}],\", \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"$$2\"}],\", etc. — and the actual values are sent to the server separately.\"]}]\n67:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"As of version 1.0.0, one of the things you can now interpolate into your tagged-template SQL queries is … tagged template SQL queries. In other words, queries are now \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"composable\"}],\" (just as they already were in libraries such as \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/porsager/postgres\",\"children\":\"postgres.js\"}],\"). This makes it easy to build up queries from smaller component parts. For example:\"]}]\n69:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"In order to support this, we’re now \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"lazy\"}],\" in transforming these tagged-template queries into SQL-with-placeholders: we do it only just before we run them. That lets us number the interpolated-value placeholders appropriately even when they’re part of a larger final query.\"]}]\n6a:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"fixing-a-potential-sql-injection-footgun\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Fixing a potential SQL-injection footgun\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#fixing-a-potential-sql-injection-footgun\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Fixing a potential SQL-injection footgun\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$57:6:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n6b:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Now, what’s the difference between these two queries?\"}]\n6d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Answer: the first is the same query we showed above. The second has an extra pair of parentheses.\"}]\n6e:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Unfortunately, before version 1.0.0, those innocuous-looking extra parentheses turned this from a safe query into a potentially very bad day: a SQL injection attack. That’s because their effect is to make the "])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"query use ordinary JavaScript string interpolation, rather than the safe tagged-template interpolation technique.\"}]\n6f:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"As of 1.0.0, the second query is a runtime error (and if you use TypeScript, it’s a type error before you even run the code).\"}]\n70:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"In earlier versions, we had deliberately made it possible to call the tagged-template query function as a conventional function to enable cases where both the query and the values are passed in as variables. In retrospect this was a mistake because, as shown above, it can make dangerous queries look terribly similar to safe ones.\"}]\n71:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"We’ve fixed the mistake and, to fill in the gap this would otherwise open up, there are two new function properties on the tagged-template function: \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"query\"}],\" and \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"unsafe\"}],\".\"]}]\n72:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"query\"}],\" function works just like the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"query\"}],\" method already found on the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"Client\"}],\" and \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"Pool\"}],\" objects (which operate over WebSockets). It lets you pass in both the query — with manually-numbered placeholders — and the values as variables:\"]}]\n74:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Second, the \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"unsafe\"}],\" function lets you embed raw SQL strings in a tagged-template query, which is OK (and sometimes necessary) if you know you can trust those raw SQL strings. For example:\"]}]\n76:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"performance-and-compatibility-when-inserting-binary-data\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit